{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgfoCrVm4mFz5F3Lfd68AM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\\label{sec:experiments}\n2\t\n3\tWe evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.\n4\t\n5\t\\paragraph{Evidence gradient.} We classify HS advantage evidence into three tiers: \\emph{cross-validated}\\footnote{In our usage, \\emph{cross-validated} means the result survives nested 5-fold cross-validation with a held-out test fold, ruling out hyperparameter overfitting. \\emph{Exploratory} ($\\dagger$) results pass HB on the holdout split but lack nested CV validation due to sample constraints.} (nested CV pass; 7--8B models), \\emph{significant} (HB pass without nested CV; QwQ-32B), and \\emph{exploratory} ($^\\dagger$, small sample or narrow coverage; R1-32B). Per-model details follow in \\cref{sec:layer-results,sec:hs-text,sec:32b-analysis}.\n6\t\n7\t\\subsection{Setup}\\label{sec:setup}\n8\t\n9\tWe study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B, trained on the OpenThoughts dataset~\\citep{openthinker}; Qwen-7B backbone, STILL-trained~\\cref{app:ot7b_details}, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \\in \\{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\\}$ selected on validation. Classification requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings (\\cref{tab:k_ablation}). Statistical testing follows \\cref{sec:evaluation}.\\looseness=-1\n10\t\n11\t\\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\\label{sec:layer-results}\n12\t\n13\t\\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the crossing-rate composite metric (balanced accuracy $\\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers across all four models: L0 for R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep; \\cref{tab:r18b_full_layer}) and OT-7B, L2 (3\\%) for QwQ-32B, and L10 (16\\%) for R1-32B (\\cref{fig:layer_heatmap}). The three threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) share a common confusion matrix and empirically converge to the same deep layer, L63 (98\\%) for both 32B models, consistent with their shared structure, though not mathematically guaranteed. Pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric, selects layers that vary across data splits and models (7--98\\% depth under 60/20/20; \\cref{app:ba_layer_comparison}), itself demonstrating that layer selection is a consequential methodological choice. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all models (\\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).\\looseness=-1\n14\t\n15\t\\begin{table}[t]\n16\t\\centering\n17\t\\small\n18\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n19\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n20\t\\toprule\n21\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{CR-comp.}$^a$ & \\textbf{Thresh.}$^b$ & $p$ \\textbf{(thresh.)} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{Prec}$^c$ \\\\\n22\t\\midrule\n23\tR1-8B & L0 (0\\%) & L14 (44\\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +20.1 \\\\\n24\tOT-7B & L0 (0\\%) & L16 (57\\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +21.9 \\\\\n25\tQwQ-32B$^\\dagger$ & L2 (3\\%) & L63 (98\\%) & ${<}0.005$ & +19.2 \\\\\n26\tR1-32B$^\\dagger$ & L10 (16\\%) & L63 (98\\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +15.4 \\\\\n27\t\\bottomrule\n28\t\\end{tabular}}\n29\t\\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\\,Crossing-rate composite (bal\\_acc $\\times$ crossing\\_rate). $^b$\\,Consensus of threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite). $^c$\\,HS$-$text precision difference (pp) at threshold-based layer (\\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). Rightmost $p$: HS vs.\\ text precision $p$-value at threshold-based layer (e.g., QwQ-32B CR-composite layer: $p{=}0.83$, non-significant). $\\dagger$\\,Sample-limited ($n \\leq 20$).}\n30\t\\label{tab:layer_selection}\n31\t\\end{table}\n32\t\n33\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n34\t\\centering\n35\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}\n36\t\\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (min-max normalized). Red dashed: CR-composite layer (bal\\_acc $\\times$ crossing\\_rate); green dashed: threshold-based layer (consensus of threshold-based metrics). For R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep), crossing rate drives CR-composite selection to L0 while threshold-based metrics retain L14. At 32B, CR-composite selections cluster near input layers while threshold-based metrics peak at deep layers.}\n37\t\\label{fig:layer_heatmap}\n38\t\\end{figure}\n39\t\n40\tThe downstream impact is severe: at CR-composite layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. The CR-composite's systematic shallow-layer selection is an artifact of cosine compression (\\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), not a genuine safety signal; random-label controls confirm this distinction (\\cref{app:random_cp}). At threshold-based layers, the HS advantage is graded: cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B, R1-8B; nested CV), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\\dagger$, and exploratory at R1-32B$^\\dagger$ (residualized gap below MDE). The $\\dagger$ marks sample-limited test sets ($n \\leq 20$) where effect sizes are descriptive rather than confirmatory. Random-label controls on QwQ-32B and R1-8B confirm that threshold-based deep-layer selection requires genuine safety labels; the geometric shallow-layer bias is Qwen-specific: retained on QwQ-32B but dissolved on R1-8B (\\cref{app:random_cp}). AUROC, excluded from our primary metrics because it is threshold-free (\\cref{sec:evaluation}), confirms HS discriminability at BA-selected layers ($\\Delta$AUROC +12.1 to +15.1pp; \\cref{tab:ba_layer_results}) but cannot diagnose the threshold-dependent layer selection bias.\\looseness=-1\n41\t\n42\t\\paragraph{Ensemble layer selection.} A three-layer ensemble that averages predicted probabilities from the precision-best, BA-best, and inverse-FPR-best layers bypasses single-metric pre-selection entirely. On the holdout split, all four models show significant HS advantage under the ensemble ($p \\leq 0.002$, HB corrected; \\cref{tab:ensemble}), including both 32B models that fail under CR-composite selection. The ensemble succeeds because averaging predictions from layers selected by different threshold-based metrics---which may favor somewhat different depths within the deep region---smooths metric-specific variation and reduces sensitivity to any single metric's layer preference. This holdout result uses the same split for both layer selection and evaluation; cross-validated confirmation is needed before the ensemble can be recommended as a general debiasing strategy (\\cref{sec:discussion}).\\looseness=-1\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{Cross-dataset replication (AdvBench).} To test whether metric-induced layer selection bias generalizes beyond HarmThoughts CP labels, we replicate the five-metric layer sweep on AdvBench~\\citep{advbench} (520 traces, 159 jailbreaks) using R1-8B with binary jailbreak/safe labels instead of step-level CP annotations. With held-out evaluation (1{,}000 bootstrap iterations, 60/40 train/test), the CR-composite selects shallow layers (median L2) while precision selects deep layers (median L14), a mean divergence of 31.7pp (95\\% CI [3.2, 48.4]pp, $p{=}0.015$) consistent with HarmThoughts (L0 vs.\\ L14). This confirms that the layer selection bias (C1) is driven by representational geometry, not label semantics; AdvBench lacks step-level CP annotations and thus does not validate the HS advantage (C2) (\\cref{app:advbench_layer_bias}).\\looseness=-1\n45\t\n46\t\\subsection{HS vs.\\ Text Comparison}\\label{sec:hs-text}\n47\t\n48\t\\begin{table}[t]\n49\t\\centering\n50\t\\small\n51\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n52\t\\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}\n53\t\\toprule\n54\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Layer} & \\textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\\Delta$\\textbf{BA} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} & \\textbf{HB} \\\\\n55\t\\midrule\n56\tR1-8B & L14 & 384d & 3 & +17.5 & +20.1 & [+12.4, +28.3] & $-$7.9 & \\checkmark \\\\\n57\t & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$7.4, +19.5] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\\\[2pt]\n58\tOT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.4, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & \\checkmark \\\\\n59\t & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+8.4, +29.0] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\checkmark \\\\[2pt]\n60\tQwQ$^\\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 5 & +13.0 & +19.2 & [+9.3, +33.5] & $-$15.0 & \\checkmark \\\\\n61\t & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$7.1, +27.7] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\\\[2pt]\n62\tR1-32B$^\\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 1 & +18.7 & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & $-$14.6 & \\checkmark \\\\\n63\t & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [$-$0.2, +24.3] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\\\\n64\t\\bottomrule\n65\t\\end{tabular}}\n66\t\\caption{HS vs.\\ text at threshold-based layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split, 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp); ``--'' = BA and FPR are not computed for the 1024d comparison because the 384d and 1024d encoders operate at different probe calibration points and do not share a decision threshold. HB: \\checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\\alpha{=}0.05$); all 384d model-level tests pass. $^\\dagger$\\,Sample-limited ($n \\leq 20$); 32B results are exploratory. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests.}\n67\t\\label{tab:hs_vs_text}\n68\t\\end{table}\n69\t\n70\tThe HS advantage is encoder-conditional: against BGE-large (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB correction (\\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Multi-seed stability appears in \\cref{app:multi_seed}. Against the 384d baseline, HS probes outperform text probes in all four models$^\\dagger$ (\\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}): cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B: +21.9pp precision, R1-8B: +20.1pp), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\\dagger$ (+19.2pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation). R1-8B FPR is directionally consistent (5/5 seeds) but not robust ($p{=}0.044$, rank 12/12 in HB step-down; 1/5 seeds Bonferroni; \\cref{app:multi_seed}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds but shows a pattern consistent with position mediation (residualized gap NS; \\cref{app:position_residual}); QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB (\\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 11.1--16.8pp above chance (\\cref{app:control}). A split-half analysis on OT-7B confirms internal validity (\\cref{app:split_half}).\\looseness=-1\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (79--92\\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage across all four models$^\\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$, all HB). The control provides partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. Random projection to 384d yields similar results ($\\Delta$Prec +8.3 to +20.5pp; \\cref{app:random_projection}), further weakening the capacity explanation. E5-Mistral-7B (4096d, PCA-reduced to 384d; \\cref{app:e5_mistral}) yields intermediate results (2/4 HB), forming a monotonic encoder-quality gradient with MiniLM (4/4) and BGE-large (1/4).\\looseness=-1\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} Against BGE-large~\\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB (1/4 precision); R1-8B shows adverse FPR direction (+1.2pp). This forms a monotonic encoder-quality gradient: MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) $\\to$ E5-Mistral-4096d$\\to$384d (2/4) $\\to$ BGE-large-1024d (1/4), with the precision advantage narrowing from +15--22pp to +6--18pp. The HS advantage is thus a function of text encoder capacity (\\cref{fig:forest_precision}); layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent.\\looseness=-1\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 9--20pp of balanced accuracy,\\footnote{R1-32B's 20.4pp drop reflects $W{=}1$; at $W{\\geq}3$, drops are 9--17pp.} but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \\cref{app:position_residual}). Three of four models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (residualized gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB corrected); R1-32B's gap is +1.9pp ($p{=}0.914$, $n{=}20$), well below the MDE of ${\\sim}$9pp and thus indistinguishable from zero at current power. Quadratic residualization (adding $(t/T)^2$) is computed for all four models: 2/4 retain significance (R1-8B +10.3pp, OT-7B +9.8pp, both HB), while R1-32B (0.0pp) and QwQ-32B ($-$1.0pp) do not (\\cref{tab:position_quadratic}). QwQ-32B's HS advantage is fully absorbed by the quadratic position term, meaning the information captured by the probe overlaps substantially with non-linear position. Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (\\cref{sec:temporal}).\\looseness=-1\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Caveats.} Nested 5-fold CV confirms the HS precision advantage at 7--8B (10/10 folds, 13--25\\% shrinkage; \\cref{app:nested_cv}); the +21.9pp OT-7B holdout estimate is an upper bound (nested CV selects L21--27 vs.\\ holdout L16). R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ (25\\% coverage); QwQ-32B lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps). FPR remains 11--50\\%, so current methods characterize the phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors.\\looseness=-1\n79\t\n80\t\\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\\label{sec:32b-analysis}\n81\t\n82\tResults are exploratory ($n{=}19$--$20$; MDE at 80\\% power: 8.6--9.5pp, \\cref{app:power_analysis}; effects below MDE marked * in \\cref{tab:32b_divergence}). Both 32B models lack nested CV; LOO-CV and multi-seed analyses (\\cref{app:layer_sweep,app:multi_seed}) provide partial robustness checks. R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\\cref{tab:32b_divergence}); replication with $n{\\geq}50$ traces is needed.\\looseness=-1\n83\t\n84\t\\begin{table}[t]\n85\t\\centering\n86\t\\small\n87\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n88\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n89\t\\toprule\n90\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Cov.} & \\textbf{Lead (steps)} & \\textbf{HB} & \\textbf{Pos.\\ margin} \\\\\n91\t\\midrule\n92\tR1-32B$^\\dagger$ & 25\\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & \\checkmark{} ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\\\\n93\tQwQ-32B$^\\dagger$ & 74\\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & \\checkmark{} (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp* \\\\\n94\t\\bottomrule\n95\t\\end{tabular}}\n96\t\\caption{32B cross-model divergence ($\\dagger$\\,$n \\leq 20$, all exploratory; MDE 8.6--9.5pp). HB: passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\\alpha{=}0.05$). Pos.\\ margin: HS BA$-$position baseline. *\\,Below MDE.}\n97\t\\label{tab:32b_divergence}\n98\t\\end{table}\n99\t\n100\tR1-32B suggests commitment 11--13 steps early (+14.2pp over position) but covers only 25\\% of traces ($W{=}1$); its precision effect (+15.4pp) falls below MDE. QwQ-32B achieves significance at all window sizes (74\\% coverage) but with negative lead time; the position baseline achieves higher BA (0.832 vs.\\ 0.796, margin +3.6pp, $p{=}0.133$, NS at $n{=}19$; \\cref{app:position_residual}). Per-model validation is needed.\\looseness=-1\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Temporal Context}\\label{sec:temporal}\n103\t\n104\tTemporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\\cref{fig:window_ablation}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\\cref{app:two_stage}). CP-aligned probe trajectories confirm sharper HS transitions at the commitment point (\\cref{fig:trajectory}); precision gaps across encoder conditions appear in \\cref{fig:forest_precision}.\\looseness=-1\n105\t\n106\t\\subsection{Negative Results}\\label{sec:negative}\n107\t\n108\tNo alternative method consistently matches supervised probing (\\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD~\\citep{adams2007bayesian}/CUSUM fail (0\\% detection on 3/4 models); Mahalanobis yields near-zero separation; RepE~\\citep{zou2023representation} is model-dependent (BA 0.756 vs.\\ 0.559); MLP probes overfit; CCS misaligns temporally; cross-model transfer fails (\\cref{app:negative_details}). The signal is directional, gradual, model-specific, and linearly separable, which excludes change-point, distance, transfer, and objective-alignment methods respectively.\\looseness=-1\n109\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e51f3ca8-7ac5-4490-8b2a-be7d501298c2","uuid":"65466a78-0168-40e0-ac34-d541ce9200a1","timestamp":"2026-05-23T11:33:33.530Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/docs/paper/sections/experiments.tex","content":"\\section{Experiments}\\label{sec:experiments}\n\nWe evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.\n\n\\paragraph{Evidence gradient.} We classify HS advantage evidence into three tiers: \\emph{cross-validated}\\footnote{In our usage, \\emph{cross-validated} means the result survives nested 5-fold cross-validation with a held-out test fold, ruling out hyperparameter overfitting. \\emph{Exploratory} ($\\dagger$) results pass HB on the holdout split but lack nested CV validation due to sample constraints.} (nested CV pass; 7--8B models), \\emph{significant} (HB pass without nested CV; QwQ-32B), and \\emph{exploratory} ($^\\dagger$, small sample or narrow coverage; R1-32B). Per-model details follow in \\cref{sec:layer-results,sec:hs-text,sec:32b-analysis}.\n\n\\subsection{Setup}\\label{sec:setup}\n\nWe study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B, trained on the OpenThoughts dataset~\\citep{openthinker}; Qwen-7B backbone, STILL-trained~\\cref{app:ot7b_details}, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \\in \\{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\\}$ selected on validation. Classification requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings (\\cref{tab:k_ablation}). Statistical testing follows \\cref{sec:evaluation}.\\looseness=-1\n\n\\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\\label{sec:layer-results}\n\n\\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the crossing-rate composite metric (balanced accuracy $\\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers across all four models: L0 for R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep; \\cref{tab:r18b_full_layer}) and OT-7B, L2 (3\\%) for QwQ-32B, and L10 (16\\%) for R1-32B (\\cref{fig:layer_heatmap}). The three threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) share a common confusion matrix and empirically converge to the same deep layer, L63 (98\\%) for both 32B models, consistent with their shared structure, though not mathematically guaranteed. Pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric, selects layers that vary across data splits and models (7--98\\% depth under 60/20/20; \\cref{app:ba_layer_comparison}), itself demonstrating that layer selection is a consequential methodological choice. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all models (\\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).\\looseness=-1\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{CR-comp.}$^a$ & \\textbf{Thresh.}$^b$ & $p$ \\textbf{(thresh.)} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{Prec}$^c$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nR1-8B & L0 (0\\%) & L14 (44\\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +20.1 \\\\\nOT-7B & L0 (0\\%) & L16 (57\\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +21.9 \\\\\nQwQ-32B$^\\dagger$ & L2 (3\\%) & L63 (98\\%) & ${<}0.005$ & +19.2 \\\\\nR1-32B$^\\dagger$ & L10 (16\\%) & L63 (98\\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +15.4 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}}\n\\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\\,Crossing-rate composite (bal\\_acc $\\times$ crossing\\_rate). $^b$\\,Consensus of threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite). $^c$\\,HS$-$text precision difference (pp) at threshold-based layer (\\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). Rightmost $p$: HS vs.\\ text precision $p$-value at threshold-based layer (e.g., QwQ-32B CR-composite layer: $p{=}0.83$, non-significant). $\\dagger$\\,Sample-limited ($n \\leq 20$).}\n\\label{tab:layer_selection}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}\n\\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (min-max normalized). Red dashed: CR-composite layer (bal\\_acc $\\times$ crossing\\_rate); green dashed: threshold-based layer (consensus of threshold-based metrics). For R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep), crossing rate drives CR-composite selection to L0 while threshold-based metrics retain L14. At 32B, CR-composite selections cluster near input layers while threshold-based metrics peak at deep layers.}\n\\label{fig:layer_heatmap}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe downstream impact is severe: at CR-composite layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. The CR-composite's systematic shallow-layer selection is an artifact of cosine compression (\\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), not a genuine safety signal; random-label controls confirm this distinction (\\cref{app:random_cp}). At threshold-based layers, the HS advantage is graded: cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B, R1-8B; nested CV), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\\dagger$, and exploratory at R1-32B$^\\dagger$ (residualized gap below MDE). The $\\dagger$ marks sample-limited test sets ($n \\leq 20$) where effect sizes are descriptive rather than confirmatory. Random-label controls on QwQ-32B and R1-8B confirm that threshold-based deep-layer selection requires genuine safety labels; the geometric shallow-layer bias is Qwen-specific: retained on QwQ-32B but dissolved on R1-8B (\\cref{app:random_cp}). AUROC, excluded from our primary metrics because it is threshold-free (\\cref{sec:evaluation}), confirms HS discriminability at BA-selected layers ($\\Delta$AUROC +12.1 to +15.1pp; \\cref{tab:ba_layer_results}) but cannot diagnose the threshold-dependent layer selection bias.\\looseness=-1\n\n\\paragraph{Ensemble layer selection.} A three-layer ensemble that averages predicted probabilities from the precision-best, BA-best, and inverse-FPR-best layers bypasses single-metric pre-selection entirely. On the holdout split, all four models show significant HS advantage under the ensemble ($p \\leq 0.002$, HB corrected; \\cref{tab:ensemble}), including both 32B models that fail under CR-composite selection. The ensemble succeeds because averaging predictions from layers selected by different threshold-based metrics---which may favor somewhat different depths within the deep region---smooths metric-specific variation and reduces sensitivity to any single metric's layer preference. This holdout result uses the same split for both layer selection and evaluation; cross-validated confirmation is needed before the ensemble can be recommended as a general debiasing strategy (\\cref{sec:discussion}).\\looseness=-1\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-dataset replication (AdvBench).} To test whether metric-induced layer selection bias generalizes beyond HarmThoughts CP labels, we replicate the five-metric layer sweep on AdvBench~\\citep{advbench} (520 traces, 159 jailbreaks) using R1-8B with binary jailbreak/safe labels instead of step-level CP annotations. With held-out evaluation (1{,}000 bootstrap iterations, 60/40 train/test), the CR-composite selects shallow layers (median L2) while precision selects deep layers (median L14), a mean divergence of 31.7pp (95\\% CI [3.2, 48.4]pp, $p{=}0.015$) consistent with HarmThoughts (L0 vs.\\ L14). This confirms that the layer selection bias (C1) is driven by representational geometry, not label semantics; AdvBench lacks step-level CP annotations and thus does not validate the HS advantage (C2) (\\cref{app:advbench_layer_bias}).\\looseness=-1\n\n\\subsection{HS vs.\\ Text Comparison}\\label{sec:hs-text}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n\\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Layer} & \\textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\\Delta$\\textbf{BA} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{Prec} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} & \\textbf{HB} \\\\\n\\midrule\nR1-8B & L14 & 384d & 3 & +17.5 & +20.1 & [+12.4, +28.3] & $-$7.9 & \\checkmark \\\\\n & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$7.4, +19.5] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\\\[2pt]\nOT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.4, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & \\checkmark \\\\\n & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+8.4, +29.0] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\checkmark \\\\[2pt]\nQwQ$^\\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 5 & +13.0 & +19.2 & [+9.3, +33.5] & $-$15.0 & \\checkmark \\\\\n & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$7.1, +27.7] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\\\[2pt]\nR1-32B$^\\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 1 & +18.7 & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & $-$14.6 & \\checkmark \\\\\n & & 1024d & & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [$-$0.2, +24.3] & \\multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}}\n\\caption{HS vs.\\ text at threshold-based layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split, 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp); ``--'' = BA and FPR are not computed for the 1024d comparison because the 384d and 1024d encoders operate at different probe calibration points and do not share a decision threshold. HB: \\checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\\alpha{=}0.05$); all 384d model-level tests pass. $^\\dagger$\\,Sample-limited ($n \\leq 20$); 32B results are exploratory. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests.}\n\\label{tab:hs_vs_text}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe HS advantage is encoder-conditional: against BGE-large (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB correction (\\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Multi-seed stability appears in \\cref{app:multi_seed}. Against the 384d baseline, HS probes outperform text probes in all four models$^\\dagger$ (\\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}): cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B: +21.9pp precision, R1-8B: +20.1pp), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\\dagger$ (+19.2pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation). R1-8B FPR is directionally consistent (5/5 seeds) but not robust ($p{=}0.044$, rank 12/12 in HB step-down; 1/5 seeds Bonferroni; \\cref{app:multi_seed}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds but shows a pattern consistent with position mediation (residualized gap NS; \\cref{app:position_residual}); QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB (\\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 11.1--16.8pp above chance (\\cref{app:control}). A split-half analysis on OT-7B confirms internal validity (\\cref{app:split_half}).\\looseness=-1\n\n\\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (79--92\\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage across all four models$^\\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$, all HB). The control provides partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. Random projection to 384d yields similar results ($\\Delta$Prec +8.3 to +20.5pp; \\cref{app:random_projection}), further weakening the capacity explanation. E5-Mistral-7B (4096d, PCA-reduced to 384d; \\cref{app:e5_mistral}) yields intermediate results (2/4 HB), forming a monotonic encoder-quality gradient with MiniLM (4/4) and BGE-large (1/4).\\looseness=-1\n\n\\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} Against BGE-large~\\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB (1/4 precision); R1-8B shows adverse FPR direction (+1.2pp). This forms a monotonic encoder-quality gradient: MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) $\\to$ E5-Mistral-4096d$\\to$384d (2/4) $\\to$ BGE-large-1024d (1/4), with the precision advantage narrowing from +15--22pp to +6--18pp. The HS advantage is thus a function of text encoder capacity (\\cref{fig:forest_precision}); layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent.\\looseness=-1\n\n\\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 9--20pp of balanced accuracy,\\footnote{R1-32B's 20.4pp drop reflects $W{=}1$; at $W{\\geq}3$, drops are 9--17pp.} but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \\cref{app:position_residual}). Three of four models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (residualized gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB corrected); R1-32B's gap is +1.9pp ($p{=}0.914$, $n{=}20$), well below the MDE of ${\\sim}$9pp and thus indistinguishable from zero at current power. Quadratic residualization (adding $(t/T)^2$) is computed for all four models: 2/4 retain significance (R1-8B +10.3pp, OT-7B +9.8pp, both HB), while R1-32B (0.0pp) and QwQ-32B ($-$1.0pp) do not (\\cref{tab:position_quadratic}). QwQ-32B's HS advantage is fully absorbed by the quadratic position term, meaning the information captured by the probe overlaps substantially with non-linear position. Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (\\cref{sec:temporal}).\\looseness=-1\n\n\\paragraph{Caveats.} Nested 5-fold CV confirms the HS precision advantage at 7--8B (10/10 folds, 13--25\\% shrinkage; \\cref{app:nested_cv}); the +21.9pp OT-7B holdout estimate is an upper bound (nested CV selects L21--27 vs.\\ holdout L16). R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ (25\\% coverage); QwQ-32B lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps). FPR remains 11--50\\%, so current methods characterize the phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors.\\looseness=-1\n\n\\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\\label{sec:32b-analysis}\n\nResults are exploratory ($n{=}19$--$20$; MDE at 80\\% power: 8.6--9.5pp, \\cref{app:power_analysis}; effects below MDE marked * in \\cref{tab:32b_divergence}). Both 32B models lack nested CV; LOO-CV and multi-seed analyses (\\cref{app:layer_sweep,app:multi_seed}) provide partial robustness checks. R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\\cref{tab:32b_divergence}); replication with $n{\\geq}50$ traces is needed.\\looseness=-1\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Cov.} & \\textbf{Lead (steps)} & \\textbf{HB} & \\textbf{Pos.\\ margin} \\\\\n\\midrule\nR1-32B$^\\dagger$ & 25\\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & \\checkmark{} ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\\\\nQwQ-32B$^\\dagger$ & 74\\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & \\checkmark{} (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp* \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}}\n\\caption{32B cross-model divergence ($\\dagger$\\,$n \\leq 20$, all exploratory; MDE 8.6--9.5pp). HB: passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\\alpha{=}0.05$). Pos.\\ margin: HS BA$-$position baseline. *\\,Below MDE.}\n\\label{tab:32b_divergence}\n\\end{table}\n\nR1-32B suggests commitment 11--13 steps early (+14.2pp over position) but covers only 25\\% of traces ($W{=}1$); its precision effect (+15.4pp) falls below MDE. QwQ-32B achieves significance at all window sizes (74\\% coverage) but with negative lead time; the position baseline achieves higher BA (0.832 vs.\\ 0.796, margin +3.6pp, $p{=}0.133$, NS at $n{=}19$; \\cref{app:position_residual}). Per-model validation is needed.\\looseness=-1\n\n\\subsection{Temporal Context}\\label{sec:temporal}\n\nTemporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\\cref{fig:window_ablation}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\\cref{app:two_stage}). CP-aligned probe trajectories confirm sharper HS transitions at the commitment point (\\cref{fig:trajectory}); precision gaps across encoder conditions appear in \\cref{fig:forest_precision}.\\looseness=-1\n\n\\subsection{Negative Results}\\label{sec:negative}\n\nNo alternative method consistently matches supervised probing (\\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD~\\citep{adams2007bayesian}/CUSUM fail (0\\% detection on 3/4 models); Mahalanobis yields near-zero separation; RepE~\\citep{zou2023representation} is model-dependent (BA 0.756 vs.\\ 0.559); MLP probes overfit; CCS misaligns temporally; cross-model transfer fails (\\cref{app:negative_details}). The signal is directional, gradual, model-specific, and linearly separable, which excludes change-point, distance, transfer, and objective-alignment methods respectively.\\looseness=-1\n","numLines":109,"startLine":1,"totalLines":109}}}
